{"question_id": "th-001", "category": "thesis", "question": "What evidence supports the thesis that sub-10B model inference costs are collapsing?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["0.85", "4.2x", "0.68"], "forbidden_keywords": [], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-edge-quant-research", "lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-orion-edge-announcement"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "Thesis spans research, pricing, hardware.", "answer": "The thesis that sub-10B model inference costs are collapsing is supported by direct pricing reductions, hardware acceleration, and structural API changes for models like LuminaSpark 1.0 (a 7B parameter model):\n- Direct API pricing drops: LuminaSpark 1.0 output pricing fell from $2.40 to $0.85 per million tokens between November 2025 and February 2026 C1 C5.\n- Hardware-driven cost reduction: The general availability of the Orion Edge NPU PCIe accelerator enables on-premises inference for these models at under $1 per million tokens C3.\n- Structural pricing shifts: API rate cards now default to quantized serving tiers, while higher-precision (fp16) tiers incur a surcharge, systematically lowering baseline inference costs C4.", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-api-pricing-nov-2025", "lumina-orion-edge-announcement", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-api-pricing-nov-2025", "lumina-orion-edge-announcement", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-spark-2-0-release"], "latency_s": 21.865395737811923, "tokens_used": 2857, "answer_correctness": 0.3333333333333333, "forbidden_violation": false, "evidence_recall_at_k": 0.6666666666666666, "citation_precision": 0.5, "citation_faithfulness": true, "temporal_correctness": null, "supersession_correctness": null, "abstention_accuracy": true}
