{"question_id": "th-003", "category": "thesis", "question": "What per-million-token inference costs does Lumina cite for quantized edge versus earlier cloud fp16 baselines?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["0.74", "3.10"], "forbidden_keywords": [], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-edge-quant-research"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "Research paper quant economics.", "answer": "Based on the provided context, Lumina cites an earlier baseline cost of $2.40 per million tokens, which was reduced to $0.85 per million tokens for its API. For quantized edge deployments (specifically LuminaSpark 1.5 running on the Orion Edge NPU), the cited amortized inference cost is $0.68 per million output tokens.", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news"], "latency_s": 15.671230312436819, "tokens_used": 2749, "answer_correctness": 0.0, "forbidden_violation": false, "evidence_recall_at_k": 0.0, "citation_precision": 0.0, "citation_faithfulness": true, "temporal_correctness": null, "supersession_correctness": null, "abstention_accuracy": true}
{"question_id": "th-004", "category": "thesis", "question": "What investable thesis do Meridian Tech Daily and Lumina funding news articulate about agent infrastructure?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["inference cost", "sub-$1", "agent"], "forbidden_keywords": [], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-inference-funding"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "News synthesis plus funding strategic rationale.", "answer": "Based on the provided context, the investable thesis centers on the collapse of small-model inference costs reshaping agent economics, driving investment and strategic deployment toward edge inference infrastructure. Specifically:\n\n- Analyst coverage highlights that dramatic reductions in model output pricing (e.g., dropping from $2.40 to $0.85 per million tokens) are fundamentally altering the economic viability and scalability of AI agents [C3].\n- This economic shift is directly fueling infrastructure capital, evidenced by Lumina AI Labs raising $180 million in a Series C round explicitly targeted at edge inference expansion [C1].\n- The thesis is further validated by commercial strategies that pair optimized small models with specialized hardware, such as exclusive agreements to bundle compressed model graphs with edge NPUs for industrial IoT deployments [C4].\n\nTogether, these sources frame low-cost, efficient small-model inference on edge hardware as the foundational infrastructure play for scalable agent economics.", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-spark-2-0-release"], "latency_s": 24.08946653828025, "tokens_used": 3199, "answer_correctness": 0.6666666666666666, "forbidden_violation": false, "evidence_recall_at_k": 1.0, "citation_precision": 1.0, "citation_faithfulness": true, "temporal_correctness": null, "supersession_correctness": null, "abstention_accu